our%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Indian+J+Nephrol%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2015 Jan-Feb;25(1):1-7.\n\ndoi: 10.4103/0971-4065.134531.\n\n# Understanding podocytopathy and its relevance to clinical nephrology\n\n[L Singh](/?term=Singh+L&cauthor_id=25684864)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\") , [G Singh](/?term=Singh+G&cauthor_id=25684864)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\") , [A K Dinda](/?term=Dinda+AK&cauthor_id=25684864)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\n\n-   PMID: **25684864**\n-   PMCID: [PMC4323905](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4323905/)\n-   DOI: [10.4103/0971-4065.134531](https://doi.org/10.4103/0971-4065.134531)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nReview\n\n# Understanding podocytopathy and its relevance to clinical nephrology\n\nL Singh et al. Indian J Nephrol. 2015 Jan-Feb.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nIndian J Nephrol\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Indian+J+Nephrol%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Indian+J+Nephrol%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2015 Jan-Feb;25(1):1-7.\n\ndoi: 10.4103/0971-4065.134531.\n\n### Authors\n\n[L Singh](/?term=Singh+L&cauthor_id=25684864)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\") , [G Singh](/?term=Singh+G&cauthor_id=25684864)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\") , [A K Dinda](/?term=Dinda+AK&cauthor_id=25684864)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\")\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, India.\n\n-   PMID: **25684864**\n-   PMCID: [PMC4323905](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4323905/)\n-   DOI: [10.4103/0971-4065.134531](https://doi.org/10.4103/0971-4065.134531)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nPodocytopathies are the most common group of glomerular disorder leading to proteinuria. On the basis of pathophysiology, light microscopic and ultrastructural evaluation, the podocytopathies include minimal change disease, diffuse mesangial sclerosis, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis and collapsing glomerulopathy. The present review summarizes the basic etiopathogenesis of podocytopthies, highlights the common genetic and acquired factors in its causation, puts forth various diagnostic modalities and discusses the role of emerging agents or treatment.\n\n**Keywords:** Collapsing glomerulopathy; diffuse mesangial sclerosis; focal segmental glomerulosclerosis; minimal change disease; podocytopathy; proteinuria.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nConflict of Interest: None declared.\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/4230/4323905/cd2db2ecd833/IJN-25-1-g002.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/4230/4323905/f2c45497040a/IJN-25-1-g002.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nSlit diaphragm. The schematic diagram…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nSlit diaphragm. The schematic diagram shows that the molecular complex of nephrin, podocin…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nSlit diaphragm. The schematic diagram shows that the molecular complex of nephrin, podocin and CD2AP forms a specialized junction between podocyte foot processes. α - Actinin four cross - links various actin filaments. The interaction between α3β1 and dystroglycans with laminin and fibronectin, respectively help in stabilization of podocyte interaction with glomerular basement membrane. Transient receptor potential cation channel 6 (TRCP6), regulates intracellular calcium in podocytes\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/4230/4323905/db9d5eb0afe6/IJN-25-1-g003.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/4230/4323905/e7290d07500a/IJN-25-1-g003.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\n(a) Photomicrograph shows minimal change…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\n(a) Photomicrograph shows minimal change disease. Glomerulus appears normal by light microscopy (silver…\n\n**Figure 2**\n\n(a) Photomicrograph shows minimal change disease. Glomerulus appears normal by light microscopy (silver methanamine, x200). (b) Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis: There is segmental sclerosis characterized by increase in mesangial matrix and obliteration of capillary lumina (silver methanamine, x200). (c) Diffuse mesangial sclerosis: There is dense sclerosis with obliteration of capillary tuft (silver methanamine, x200). (d) Collapsing glomerulopathy: There is collapse of glomerular tuft and hyperplasia of overlying visceral epithelial cells. An occasional resorption droplets are also noted (silver methanamine, x200)\n\n [![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/4230/4323905/72f054efdbcf/IJN-25-1-g004.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/4230/4323905/9faf3fd0a443/IJN-25-1-g004.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\n(a) Transmission electron microscope photomicrograph…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\n(a) Transmission electron microscope photomicrograph shows a normal podocyte foot process (x2100). (b)…\n\n**Figure 3**\n\n(a) Transmission electron microscope photomicrograph shows a normal podocyte foot process (x2100). (b) Minimal change disease with extensive foot process effacement with microvillus transformation. Glomerular basement membrane is normal and there are no electron dense deposits (x2100)\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Schnaper HW, Robson AM, Kopp JB. Nephrotic syndrome: Minimal change disease, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, and collapsing glomerulopathy. In: Schrier RW, editor. Diseases of the Kidney and Urinary Tract. 8th ed. Philadelphia: Lippincott Williams and Wilkins; 2006. pp. 1585–672.\n2.  1.  Pollak MR. Inherited podocytopathies: FSGS and nephrotic syndrome from a genetic viewpoint. J Am Soc Nephrol. 2002;13:3016–23. - [PubMed](/12444222/)\n3.  1.  Tryggvason K, Wartiovaara J. Molecular basis of glomerular permselectivity. Curr Opin Nephrol Hypertens. 2001;10:543–9. - [PubMed](/11458036/)\n4.  1.  Kriz W, Hackenthal E, Nobiling R, Sakai T, Elger M, Hähnel B. A role for podocytes to counteract capillary wall distension. Kidney Int. 1994;45:369–76. - [PubMed](/8164421/)\n5.  1.  St John PL, Abrahamson DR. Glomerular endothelial cells and podocytes jointly synthesize laminin-1 and-11 chains. Kidney Int. 2001;60:1037–46. - [PubMed](/11532098/)\n\nShow all 56 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Review\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Review%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Review)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n\n## LinkOut - more resources\n\n-   ### Full Text Sources\n    \n    -   [Europe PMC](https://europepmc.org/abstract/MED/25684864)\n    -   [Medknow Publications and Media Pvt Ltd](http://www.indianjnephrol.org/article.asp?issn=0971-4065;year=2015;volume=25;issue=1;spage=1;epage=7;aulast=Singh)\n    -   [Ovid Technologies, Inc.](https://www.ovid.com/25684864.pmid)\n    -   [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/pmid/25684864/)\n-   ### Other Literature Sources\n    \n    -   [scite Smart Citations](https://scite.ai/reports/25684864)\n